ecurity forces sometimes people get  taken away by the security forces and  they turn up dead the next day  the sunta is just terrorizing the  civilian population  david talks are underway that might lead  to the resistance having its own army  to fight myanmar's military head-on can  you explain how this could work and  where the troops for this resistance  army would come from  sure so the first thing i have to do is  give you a little bit of context on the  country  in the outer reaches of myanmar around  the periphery you've got  ethnic minority states and these ethnic  minority states are tired  of having a highly centralized  government that's dominated by the  majority  ethnic group the burmese and this highly  centralized government being able to  call the shots across the country  and these ethnic minority states they  have long wanted more autonomy and more  say over their own  affairs and there's actually ethnic  armed groups  with weapons here that have been  fighting battles against the myanmar  army  for decades uh to try and give their  respective states more autonomy  well now discussions are underway that  could potentially lead to bringing these  different armed ethnic groups together  into one sort of national resistance  army  now there's roughly two dozen of these  ethernet groups  depending on how many of them you can  get to pull together  and sign on to the sort of national  resistance army you could potentially  be looking at a sort of resistance force  of about a hundred thousand maybe plus  whoever they could recruit from the  civilian population on top of that  they would still be way outnumbered by  the myanmar army which has more than  four hundred thousand  and it's better resourced um but still a  hundred thousand  could still cause a lot of problems for  the myanmar army now i should say  there's still a lot of discussions and a  lot of details that need to be worked  out  before this sort of unified resistance  army could come toget